  "issue_number": 10835,
  "issue_title": "Regression: task failure handling is interrupted for chains without a result backend",
  "issue_body": "<!--\nPlease fill this template entirely and do not erase parts of it.\nWe reserve the right to close without a response\nbug reports which are incomplete.\n-->\n\n# Checklist\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [ ] This has already been asked to the [discussions forum](https://github.com/celery/celery/discussions) first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the\n  [contribution guide](https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/main/contributing.html#other-bugs)\n  on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the [issues list](https://github.com/celery/celery/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22Issue+Type%3A+Bug+Report%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the [pull requests list](https://github.com/celery/celery/pulls?q=is%3Apr+label%3A%22PR+Type%3A+Bugfix%22+-label%3A%22Category%3A+Documentation%22)\n  for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the [commit log](https://github.com/celery/celery/commits/main)\n  to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues\n  in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [ ] I have tried to reproduce the issue with [pytest-celery](https://docs.celeryq.dev/projects/pytest-celery/en/latest/userguide/celery-bug-report.html) and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue (relevant packages).\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required\n  to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version\n  and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or\n  result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message\n  broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries,\n  ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading\n  and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies (works on 5.7.0a1, broken on 5.7.0b1).\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- #10767 \u2014 \"Fail the chords after a chord whose header failed\". It is the PR that moved\n  the chain walk out of `mark_as_failure()` into `_fail_chain()`, and it is the most likely\n  origin of this regression (see \"Regression\" below).\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None found. I searched issues and PRs for `_fail_chain`, `DisabledBackend` + chain,\n  `No result backend is configured` + chain, `get_state` + chain, and\n  chain + `on_failure` / errback / `task_failure` created after 2026-10-02.\n  #10783, #10784 and #10778 concern chord errbacks and are a different problem.\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.7.0b1 (`celery/backends/base.py` is identical to upstream `main` as of 2026-10-10)\n\n**Python version**: 3.12.3 (CPython, Linux)\n\n**Broker**: `memory://`\n\n**Result backend**: none configured (`DisabledBackend`)\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.7.0b1 (collider) kombu:5.7.0b1 py:3.12.3\n            billiard:4.3.1 memory:N/A\nplatform -> system:Linux arch:64bit, ELF\n            kernel version:6.18.44-fc-v114 imp:CPython\nloader   -> celery.loaders.app.AppLoader\nsettings -> transport:memory results:disabled\n\nbroker_url: 'memory://:********@localhost//'\ndeprecated_settings: None\nworker_hijack_root_logger: False\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n* **Minimal Python Version**: 3.12.3 (not bisected lower)\n* **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.7.0b1 (regression; 5.7.0a1 is fine)\n* **Minimal Kombu Version**: 5.7.0b1\n* **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A (in-memory broker)\n* **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A (no result backend; this is the trigger)\n* **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: Linux 6.18 (other OSes not tested)\n* **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A\n* **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output (relevant packages):</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\namqp==5.4.0\nbilliard==4.3.1\ncelery==5.7.0b1\nclick==8.5.0\nkombu==5.7.0b1\npython-dateutil==2.9.0.post0\ntzdata==2026.4\nvine==5.1.0\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\nNone. No external broker or result backend is needed.\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nSelf-contained: it starts an in-process worker, so it runs with a single `python repro.py`.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\nfrom celery import Celery, Task, chain, signals\nfrom celery.contrib.testing.worker import start_worker\n\n# Broker only: no result backend is configured (DisabledBackend).\napp = Celery(\"proj\", broker=\"memory://\")\napp.conf.worker_hijack_root_logger = False\nseen = []\n\n\nclass Base(Task):\n    def on_failure(self, exc, task_id, args, kwargs, einfo):\n        seen.append(f\"Task.on_failure() called: {exc!r}\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.boom\", base=Base)\ndef boom():\n    raise ValueError(\"boom\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.after\")\ndef after(x=None):\n    seen.append(\"after ran\")\n\n\n@app.task(name=\"proj.alert\")\ndef alert(*args, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(\"errback (link_error) ran\")\n\n\n@signals.task_failure.connect\ndef on_task_failure(sender=None, exception=None, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(f\"task_failure signal fired: {exception!r}\")\n\n\n@signals.task_internal_error.connect\ndef on_internal_error(sender=None, exception=None, **kwargs):\n    seen.append(f\"task_internal_error signal: {exception!r}\")\n\n\nwith start_worker(app, pool=\"solo\", perform_ping_check=False,\n                  loglevel=\"CRITICAL\", shutdown_timeout=10):\n    chain(boom.s(), after.s()).on_error(alert.si()).apply_async()\n    time.sleep(3)\n\nprint(\"\\n\".join(seen) or \"(nothing happened: no log, no signal, no on_failure, no errback)\")\n```\n\n**Output on 5.7.0a1**\n\n```\nerrback (link_error) ran\nTask.on_failure() called: ValueError('boom')\ntask_failure signal fired: ValueError('boom')\n```\n\n**Output on 5.7.0b1 / `main`**\n\n```\ntask_internal_error signal: NotImplementedError('No result backend is configured.\\nPlease see the documentation for more information.')\ntask_internal_error signal: NotImplementedError('No result backend is configured.\\nPlease see the documentation for more information.')\n```\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nA chain does not need a result backend, so its failure handling must not need one either.\nWhen a task in a chain fails, Celery should:\n\n* call `Task.on_failure()`;\n* send the `task_failure` signal;\n* run the `link_error` errbacks;\n* log `Task ... raised unexpected: ...`.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nWhen a non-last task of a chain fails and no result backend is configured, none of the above happens.\nThe worker only emits `task_internal_error` (twice) with `NotImplementedError: No result backend is configured.`\nNo error is logged for the failed task, and no errback, `on_failure` or `task_failure` handler runs.\n\n| Failure handling              | 5.7.0a1 | 5.7.0b1 / `main`  |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- | ----------------- |\n| `link_error` errback          | runs    | **never runs**    |\n| `Task.on_failure()`           | called  | **never called**  |\n| `task_failure` signal         | fired   | **never fired**   |\n| \"raised unexpected\" error log | logged  | **not logged**    |\n\nAnything that relies on `task_failure` (for example error-tracking integrations) or on `link_error`\nsees nothing. In a worker this is a silent loss of failure handling.\n\n## Root Cause\n\n`BaseBackend._fail_chain()` (`celery/backends/base.py`) now reads the state of every chain element\nbefore propagating the failure:\n\n```python\nalready_failed = (\n    chain_elem_ctx.id is not None and\n    self.get_state(chain_elem_ctx.id) in states.PROPAGATE_STATES\n)\n```\n\nWithout a result backend, `self` is a `DisabledBackend`, whose `get_state` is `_is_disabled` and\nraises `NotImplementedError`. `mark_as_failure()` calls `_fail_chain()` before it fires the task's\nerrbacks, so the exception escapes `handle_failure()` and everything after it is skipped.\n\n`already_failed` is only used for chord members (`'chord' in chain_elem_ctx.options`). A chord requires\na result backend, so a disabled backend never needs this read. On a real backend it is also a needless\nround trip per remaining chain element on every failure.\n\n## Regression\n\nWorks on 5.7.0a1, broken on 5.7.0b1. `_fail_chain()` was introduced in #10767, which is the likely\norigin. I could not confirm which commit added the `get_state` line.\n\n## Possible Fix\n\nOnly read the state for chord members. It must still be read before the failure is stored:\n\n```diff\n+            # Only a chord member needs this: it is what keeps its part from\n+            # being returned twice.  Reading the state is also left to chord\n+            # members because a chord needs a result backend, whereas a plain\n+            # chain does not, and a disabled backend refuses to be read.\n+            # It must be read before the failure is stored below.\n             already_failed = (\n+                'chord' in chain_elem_ctx.options and\n                 chain_elem_ctx.id is not None and\n                 self.get_state(chain_elem_ctx.id) in states.PROPAGATE_STATES\n             )\n```\n\nI have a patch with regression tests (backend-level and an end-to-end tracer test) and will open a PR\nlinked to this issue.",
